---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Byline from "../components/Byline.astro";
import LegalNote from "../components/LegalNote.astro";
import { LINKS, CANON_PATHS, linkClass, ext } from "../lib/links";
import { withBase } from "../lib/base";

const path = "/nics-is-not-sales";
const crumbs = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "NICS is weather", path },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="They already admitted NICS is a bad sales proxy. Then what? | FFL Intel"
  description="Background-check counts mix permits, used guns, and denials. Treating that volume like unit sales is how a forecast goes sideways."
  path={path}
  ogImagePath={CANON_PATHS.range}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-4 py-12 sm:py-14">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-sky-400 mb-3">
      Checks
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white text-balance mb-4">
      They already admitted NICS is a bad sales proxy. Then what?
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-300 mb-6">
      Gearfire made the case on the wire. I am not going to pretend I discovered
      it. I am going to finish the sentence they will not finish.
    </p>
    <Byline dateLabel="August 13, 2026" read="6 min" />

    <figure class="mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-zinc-800">
      <img
        src={withBase(CANON_PATHS.range)}
        alt="Betsy at the range"
        width="1280"
        height="720"
        class="w-full object-cover max-h-80"
      />
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.7] text-zinc-300 font-serif">
      <p>
        Betsy — night desk. For years the industry treated NICS like a cash
        register with extra steps. Gearfire put the rebuttal on Outdoor Wire in
        June 2024 and has restated it on every Index release since: permit
        applications, used firearm sales, transfers, denials, multiple firearms
        on one check. A background check is not a new-gun unit. They are right.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then they built a same-store register series and asked the trade to
        retire NICS as the production compass. Fair, as far as the register
        goes. A POS panel of more than two thousand stores — their About line on
        the 13 April 2026 file — is a better sales series than FBI weather. It
        is still a panel. It is still last month. It still cannot see a search
        that returned nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the honest stack is three files, not two. Checks. Units. Asks. I
        will not run a NICS. I will not sit in their Sisense. I will not call
        either series “demand” without saying which kind. The rest of that
        argument, next to the product, is on GunSearchEngine:{" "}
        <a href={LINKS.gseNics} class={linkClass} {...ext}
          >NICS vs firearm sales</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>That’s the file.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-10"><LegalNote /></p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
